Overview
The objective of this work was to transform a static Solar Panel Production Line simulation model in AnyLogic into a smart, flexible, and self-regulating Industrial 4.0 system. By integrating data-driven AI insights (predictions from an embedded LSTM neural network model via ONNX Runtime) with the physical execution layers of the conveyor network, the project successfully broke the isolation between individual machine processes, establishing a closed-loop "Predict-Decide-Execute" system.

The optimization focuses on the middle-to-end sequence of the manufacturing line, specifically coordinating four tightly coupled process areas: Feeding EVA & Backsheet, Buffer Queue, Lamination, and Cooling.

Key Work & Technical Achievements
1. Smart Inflow Regulation: Feeding EVA Second Layer and Backsheet
Previous State: Operated under a rigid, fixed processing time, blindly pushing materials forward regardless of downstream backup, which frequently caused physical overflow and line blockages.

Smart Solution: Decoupled the station's Process time by binding it to a dynamic variable feedingEVASheetTime. When the AI script (testEvent) detects a high congestion risk, it automatically throttles the inflow by extending the delay to 20.0 seconds (restoring to 10.0 seconds under clear conditions). This proactive gating gives downstream machines the necessary buffer time to clear their inventory.

2. Adaptive Capacity Strategy: Buffer Queue
Previous State: Configured with Maximum capacity (infinite capacity), which unrealistically masked bottleneck points and bypassed physical workspace limitations.

Smart Solution: Disabled the infinite capacity option and locked the standard baseline capacity to 20 units. Integrated the programmatic queue.set_capacity() method inside the AI evaluation loop. If a high congestion probability is predicted and the buffer length crosses a critical threshold, the queue dynamically expands its limit to 35 units. This accurately simulates the open-floor logic of spinning up a temporary "backup staging area" in a digital twin scenario.

3. Dynamic Process Throttling: Lamination Station
Previous State: Configured with a static processing time, acting as the primary bottleneck of the entire line and failing to cope with sudden upstream batch surges.

Smart Solution: Replaced the hardcoded time value in the ConveyorSimpleStation with a dynamic function call, getLaminatorCycleTime(). Controlled by the global parameter evaType, the laminator now switches its operation modes based on AI risk assessments. In times of severe congestion, it forces a transition into a high-throughput "Ultra-Fast Cure" or "Fast Cure" mode, drastically shortening cycles to aggressively deplete the buffer queue.

4. Physical Attribute Interlocking: Cooling Station
Previous State: Maintained a strict, unyielding 30-second cooling cycle, entirely detached from the variable heat and cross-linking levels coming out of the laminator.

Smart Solution: Overhauled the station's Process time with a nested ternary conditional expression: (evaType == 2) ? 20.0 : ((evaType == 1) ? 30.0 : 45.0). This establishes a direct physical correlation between lamination recipes and cooling times. Shorter lamination cycles (less residual heat) require less cooling (20s), while a full high-quality standard cure receives an extended 45s cooling down period, ensuring optimal panel integrity before framing and quality checks.

System Integration & Code Refactoring (testEvent)
To support the physical layout adjustments, a total overhaul of the central control trigger, testEvent (executing periodically every 10 seconds), was conducted:

Eliminated Java Scope Conflicts: Resolved critical compilation issues (Syntax errors) caused by duplicate local variable declarations of prob and currentLength. The code was streamlined into a single, clean scope block.

Three-Tier Control Matrix: Implemented a structured if-else hierarchy defining High Risk (Alert), Medium Risk (Warning), and Safe/Clear (Restore) states, enabling smooth parameter transition curves without destabilizing the discrete-event engine.

Production-Ready Logging: Removed all graphic emojis and non-standard characters from the console output logs to guarantee robust, crash-free execution (traceln) across different OS runtimes, terminal character encodings, and version control branches.
